Notes on OPD-III Nidek files

There are uncompressed ASCII readable files and binary files.

from the manual and brochure:
33 vertical, 39 horizontal (6 extra partial horizontal rings horizontally)
11,880 points and more
Axial, Instantaneous, "Refractive", Elevation
33 x 360 = 11880

The ASCII files:

They are similar to EyeSys dat files: RA, XX, PU, HDR 
RA == ?Radii of rings in EyeSys terms, increasing in magnitude from 40's to 400's with the EyeSys XX files being the sagittal curvature given in diopters 700's because R=(n-1)/D
EyeSys EY* files are binary and unknown.

There are only 23 mires in the ASCII files; the manual says the number of rings is adjustable up to 33
Nidek RA seems to be 7.xx curvatures (corresponding to EyeSys XX file) 
ED 0.40 to 4.x ? radii (corresponding to EyeSys RA file) 
but ED*.DAT has 3 sets of numbers, the second two sets are preceded with labels AFTER DB_V3, AFTER DB_V4
some are negative as well, but only in the "after DB_V4" section.
currently using first set of 360 not "AFTER DB_V3, AFTER DB_V4"
PR/PR files appear to be pupil diameters: using PE
HT* files seem to be elevation from 0.01- > 2.x
QV* files are Q values for CL fitting per the manual
EY* files are binary and unknown.

The binary files:

The binary calibration files may simply be compressed; they appear to be a 30-50% smaller than the uncompressed/ASCII versions & the opd-iii manual describes 
in the Import/Export of Data on p.263 also ratio of compression is small in the sample shown also p.266 "MultiByte" setting on Export
However, if so, not very good nonstandard compression, for example AR*.DAT files ASCII are about 25Kbytes, binary versions are 14Kybes but using gzip on ASCII gives 3.7Kbytes

The compressed/binary files appear to have 33 mires and 360 meridians (each degree)
They are calibration files, not patient data, stored as *.CAB files under Windows, extracted and all with *.DAT except for RP*.txt
RE*, PU*, PP*, EY* .DAT are all *.PNG image files, EY* are image files of the mires. There are 16 rings in an image seen, so 32 edges.
RP*.txt viewed in mc has hex data terminated with ASCII descriptors of cylinder, sphere, PD etc. look like summary data; line terminations are (0D 0A)
HD*. dat is almost certainly summary data and patient information similar to Export data function; line terminations are (0D 0A)

The Model Eye for calibration has 8.00 mm curvature p.280

